      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The arrival of the East Line and Red River Railroad in the late 1870s turned Leesburg into a vital station on the line.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/leesburg-tx",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A post office opened in Leesburg in the 1850s, marking the town’s early growth as a regional hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Leesburg,_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Cotton farming in Leesburg declined in the 1920s after boll weevil infestations and falling prices hit the region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Leesburg,_Texas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Today Leesburg still operates a post office and its economy reflects Camp County’s shift from farming to timber and light manufacturing.",
